{"records":[{"id":"j5n84znyvf","title":"Tumor-Educated Hepatocytes Drive Immune Evasion in Colorectal Cancer Liver Metastasis Through KLK5 Secretion","description":"1.RNA-seq data generated from paired primary colorectal cancer lesions and matched hepatic metastatic lesions obtained from the same patients with colorectal cancer liver metastasis (CRLM).\n2.RNA-seq data of THLE-2 cells after co-culture with RKO cells and of control (monocultured) THLE-2 cells.\n3. RNA-seq data of RKO cells after co-culture with THLE-2 cells and of control (monocultured) RKO cells.\n4. LC–MS data for protein identification in conditioned-medium supernatants from control THLE-2 cells and tumor-educated THLE-2 cells.\n5. CRISPR screening for KLK5 substrates.\n6. List of sgRNA sequences used for CRISPR screening.\n7. LC–MS data for the identification of lysine lactylation sites on PD-L1 protein.\n8. LC–MS data for the identification of lysine lactylation sites on PD-L1 protein.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-22T17:55:50.043Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j5n84znyvf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j5n84znyvf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jiaxi","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Metastatic Colorectal Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3k99jn38w3","title":"Catecholaminergic cell type-specific expression of Cre recombinase in knock-in transgenic rats generated by the Combi-CRISPR technology","description":"TH-Cre KI rat strains produced by the Combi-CRISPR system offer a beneficial model to study the molecular and cellular mechanics for the morphogenesis, physiology, and pathology of catecholamine-containing neurons in the brain.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-09-30T15:25:43.267Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3k99jn38w3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3k99jn38w3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shigeki","last_name":"Kato","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kazuto","last_name":"Kobayashi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yoshio","last_name":"Iguchi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Natsuki","last_name":"Matsushita","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hshkzhjy3x","title":"MC Kurihara et al. (2022)","description":"Image data related to Kurihara et al., Structure of the type V-C CRISPR-Cas effector enzyme. Mol Cell (2022)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-30T09:01:51.071Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hshkzhjy3x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hshkzhjy3x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nina","last_name":"Kurihara","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2vcpns9jgw","title":"Small-molecule and CRISPR screening converge to reveal RTK dependencies in pediatric rhabdoid tumors. Oberlick and Rees et al., 2019. ","description":"Raw data underlying several figures from our article in Cell Reports:\nOberlick and Rees et al., Small-Molecule and CRISPR Screening Converge to Reveal Receptor Tyrosine Kinase Dependencies in Pediatric Rhabdoid Tumors, Cell Reports (2019), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2019.07.021","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-08-27T12:00:11.138Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2vcpns9jgw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2vcpns9jgw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elaine ","last_name":"Oberlick","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pediatric Cancer","Chromatin Remodeling","Rhabdoid Tumor","Screening","Bioactive Compound","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f5kx9k264n","title":"Cell-type-directed network-correcting combination therapy for Alzheimer’s disease. Yaqiao Li et al.","description":"We organized and computationally integrated a comprehensive single nucleus RNA-sequencing (snRNA-seq) dataset by combining published data from three independent studies ( Mathys et al 2019., Zhou et al. 2020, and Lau et al. 2020). This integrated analysis enhanced patient representation, effectively corrected batch/technical artifacts, and improved consistency in DEG detection efficiency across cell types, enabling robust and unbiased downstream drug target screening using these cell-type-specific DEG profiles as inputs. \n\nSingle-nucleus RNA-sequencing analysis of the combination-treated (letrozole and irinotecan) and vehicle-treated mice confirmed that the combination therapy demonstrated beneficial effects transcriptomically, through increasing neuronal cell abundance, promoting neuroprotective functional pathways, and reversing disease-associated gene networks in a cell-type-specific manner.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-15T17:29:21.924Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f5kx9k264n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f5kx9k264n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yaqiao","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Single-Cell RNA Sequencing","Single-Cell Transcriptomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r7fgtnxcb5","title":"MC Takeda et al. (2020)","description":"Image data related to Takeda et al., Structure of the miniature type V-F CRISPR-Cas effector enzyme. Mol Cell (2020)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-18T10:52:11.746Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r7fgtnxcb5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r7fgtnxcb5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Satoru","last_name":"Takeda","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y4bg25s5w5","title":"Kato, Zhou et al. Cell (2022) ","description":"Image data related to Kato, Zhou et al., Structure and engineering of the type III-E CRISPR-Cas7-11 effector complex. Cell (2022)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-30T09:01:54.003Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y4bg25s5w5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y4bg25s5w5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hiroshi","last_name":"Nishimasu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"97sdcb5273","title":"A Single-copy Knock In Translating Ribosome ImmunoPrecipitation tool kit for tissue specific profiling of actively translated mRNAs in C. elegans. Wester and Lanjuin et al.","description":"Translating Ribosome Affinity Purification (TRAP) methods have emerged as a powerful approach to profile actively translated transcripts in specific cell and tissue types. Epitope tagged ribosomal subunits are expressed in defined cell populations and used to pull down ribosomes and their associated mRNAs, providing a snapshot of cell-type specific translation occurring in that space and time. Current TRAP toolkits available to the C. elegans community have been built using multi-copy arrays, randomly integrated in the genome. Here we introduce a Single-copy Knock In Translating Ribosome ImmunoPrecipitation (SKI TRIP) tool kit, a collection of C. elegans strains engineered by CRISPR in which tissue specific expression of FLAG tagged ribosomal subunit protein RPL-22 is driven by cassettes present in single copy from defined sites in the genome. In depth characterization of the SKI TRIP strains and methodology shows that 3xFLAG tagged RPL-22 expressed from its endogenous locus or within defined cell types incorporates into actively translating ribosomes and can be used to efficiently and cleanly pull down cell-type specific transcripts without impacting overall mRNA translation or fitness of the animal. We propose SKI TRIP use for the study of processes that are acutely sensitive to changes in translation, such as aging.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-31T06:32:34.617Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/97sdcb5273","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/97sdcb5273"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Wester","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anne","last_name":"Lanjuin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Emanuel","last_name":"Bruckisch","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maria","last_name":"Perez Matos","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"Stine","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Heintz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Denzel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"William","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Caenorhabditis elegans","Immunoprecipitation","Ribosome","Cellular Aging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2jdyyyd8hx","title":"Cell type specific binocular interactions","description":"Projections from each eye are segregated in separate domains within the dorsal lateral geniculate nucleus (dLGN). Yet, in vivo studies indicate that the activity of single dLGN neurons can be influenced by visual stimuli presented to either eye. In this study we explored whether intrinsic circuits mediate binocular interactions in the mouse dLGN. We employed dual color optogenetics in vitro to selectively activate input from each eye and recorded synaptic responses in thalamocortical (relay) cells as well as inhibitory interneurons, which have extensive dendritic arbors that are not confined to eye specific domains.  While most relay cells received monocular retinal input, most interneurons received binocular retinal input and most relay cells received binocular retinogeniculate-evoked inhibition. In recordings from closely-spaced pairs of relay cells and interneurons, the most common relationship observed was retinogeniculate excitation of interneurons paired with inhibition of relay cells via activation of inputs from the same eye(s). Moreover, relay cells that received binocular inhibition were located in and around the ipsilateral patch of retinogeniculate terminals, suggesting that interneuron output is spatially related to retinal input. Finally, we found that dLGN interneurons are interconnected, displaying both monocular and binocular inhibition in response to retinal activation. In sum, our results indicate that geniculate interneurons provide one of the first locations where signals from the two eyes can be compared, integrated, and adjusted before being transmitted to cortex, shedding new light on the role of the thalamus in binocular vision.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-29T07:37:04.396Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2jdyyyd8hx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2jdyyyd8hx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sean","last_name":"Masterson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martha","last_name":"Bickford","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuroscience","Retina","Synapse","Thalamus","Optogenetics","Vision"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":126006,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":157,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":126006,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":125514,"restricted":62,"embargoed":430},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2132},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1452},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":610},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":451},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":423},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":393},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":390},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":354},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":314},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":265}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":123417},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1893},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":182},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":128},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":71},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":63},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":52},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":39},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":945},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":864},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":477},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":404},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":358}]}}